WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.960
A gradient can bring an axon to the right neighbourhood.

2
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.520
But a thin layer is either the right one, or it is not.

3
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:18.180
In the retina, each cell type sends its processes into one or a few sublaminae.

4
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.340
How does a process choose?

5
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.120
Discrete choices call for discrete labels.

6
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:33.243
In chick retina, two proteins, Sidekick-1 and Sidekick-2, are made by largely separate

7
00:00:33.243 --> 00:00:34.243
groups of neurons.

8
00:00:35.323 --> 00:00:38.923
The processes of each group end in different sublaminae.

9
00:00:40.043 --> 00:00:44.523
Each protein binds its own kind, which is called homophilic binding.

10
00:00:45.943 --> 00:00:50.343
Cells carrying Sidekick-1 stick to other cells carrying Sidekick-1.

11
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:56.777
The key test was misexpression, which means making a protein in a cell that

12
00:00:56.777 --> 00:00:58.177
normally lacks it.

13
00:00:58.977 --> 00:01:03.257
Retinal neurons were made to produce a Sidekick they did not normally make.

14
00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:08.717
Their processes shifted toward the sublamina where that Sidekick was already

15
00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:13.017
concentrated. So a label biases where an arbor ends.

16
00:01:13.737 --> 00:01:16.897
It is one part of how layers form, not the whole.

17
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.650
Now the opposite problem.

18
00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:25.310
A neuron must keep its own branches apart, which is called self-avoidance.

19
00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:31.810
In the fly, the protein Dscam1 also binds mainly to identical forms.

20
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:38.390
But here the outcome is repulsion, so two touching branches with the same forms pull

21
00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:45.090
apart. Without Dscam1, fly sensory neurons have dendrites that cross and bundle with

22
00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.090
their own branches.

23
00:01:46.883 --> 00:01:52.423
To tell itself from its neighbours, each neuron needs its own surface identity.

24
00:01:53.423 --> 00:02:00.863
A single fly gene, Dscam1, can be spliced into 38,016 forms.

25
00:02:01.743 --> 00:02:08.243
Each neuron appears to express a small, partly random subset, and a large menu keeps

26
00:02:08.243 --> 00:02:09.803
accidental matches rare.

27
00:02:09.803 --> 00:02:16.183
Its own branches match, so they pull apart, while branches of other neurons do not

28
00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:17.543
match and can overlap.

29
00:02:18.433 --> 00:02:25.413
Gradients give continuous order, while labels give discrete membership, such as a

30
00:02:25.413 --> 00:02:27.593
layer, or self versus other.

31
00:02:28.593 --> 00:02:31.293
The same chemistry can serve opposite ends.

32
00:02:32.253 --> 00:02:34.933
Matching Sidekick draws processes together.

33
00:02:35.773 --> 00:02:38.553
Matching Dscam1 drives branches apart.

34
00:02:39.553 --> 00:02:42.933
What the cell does after the bond forms decides which.
